    "evidence_text": "The descent of memory T-cell subsets\n\nThe generation of subsets of memory T cells with distinct homing and functional properties increases our defensive capabilities.\n\nHowever, the developmental relationship of memory T-cell subsets is a matter of debate.\n\nTissue-resident memory T cells: local specialists in immune defence\n\nT cells have crucial roles in protection against infection and cancer.\n\nResponse to self antigen imprints regulatory memory in tissues\n\nIn fact, these diseases often spontaneously resolve, despite persistent tissue autoantigen expression.\n\nThus, Treg cells function to confer \u2018regulatory memory\u2019 to the target tissue.\n\nMolecular regulation of effector and memory T cell differentiation\n\nMaintaining the norm: T-cell homeostasis\n\nTherefore, population maintenance of naive Th cells must be at least partly peripherally based.\n\nT cell memory.\n\nT cell memory induced by prior infection or vaccination provides enhanced protection against subsequent microbial infections.\n\nThe persistence of the resting memory T cells that eventually develop is regulated by cytokines.\n\nThis chapter discusses recent findings on how memory T cells develop to confer long-term protective immunity.\n\nIn humans, however, the early development and function of T cells in tissues remain unexplored.\n\nCentral memory and effector memory T cell subsets: function, generation, and maintenance.",
